{"kind":"report","text":"Design advice for the bounded Tailapp runtime metrics contract, reviewed against published main f14a79fdb97e09d791b213b16cec1932d424574a.\n\nVERDICT: approve the local in-process aggregate boundary with the required refinements below. CLI/MCP-only snapshots, no retained request events, no Prometheus endpoint, and no self-export are the right v1 scope.\n\nREQUIRED 1 — define one stable snapshot schema and reset epoch. Partition the response into process aggregates, durable/current engine state, and Go runtime gauges. Include schema_version, process_started_at_unix_nano, snapshot_at_unix_nano, and uptime_milliseconds. All counters and histograms must say “since this resident started” and reset on restart; inbox/frontier gauges and the existing projection tailapp_stats values survive restart and reset only under their own documented lifecycle. This prevents a consumer from treating a restart as negative traffic. Sort metric families and label tuples deterministically. Prefer decimal strings for cumulative uint64 values, or explicitly cap them below the JSON exact-integer limit.\n\nREQUIRED 2 — use closed names, labels, outcomes, and exact timing boundaries. Suggested families are tailapp_otlp_requests_total, tailapp_otlp_records_accepted_total, tailapp_otlp_canonical_bytes_accepted_total, tailapp_otlp_request_duration_milliseconds; tailapp_projection_delivery_attempts_total, tailapp_projection_queue_delay_milliseconds, tailapp_projection_processing_duration_milliseconds; tailapp_queries_total, tailapp_query_rows_returned_total, tailapp_query_results_truncated_total, tailapp_query_result_bytes_total, tailapp_query_duration_milliseconds; and tailapp_control_operations_total plus tailapp_control_operation_duration_milliseconds. Signal is exactly log/span/metric/unknown. Control operation is mapped through the fixed dispatch table, with unknown rather than raw input. Outcome is a closed enum, never err.Error or HTTP text. For OTLP, reuse stable receiver codes such as accepted, receiver_busy, method_not_allowed, unsupported_content_type, request_too_large, invalid_otlp, invalid_record, too_many_records, inbox_full, ingestion_not_ready, persist_failed, and not_found. For queries, distinguish success, truncated_success, not_found, unavailable, frontier_changed, budget_exceeded, cancelled, invalid_request, and operation_failed. For delivery attempts, distinguish applied, already_applied, deterministic_gap, retryable_error, settlement_error, and detach_error; do not call attempts “records consumed,” because a successful projection commit followed by queue-settlement failure is retried as AlreadyApplied.\n\nStart OTLP request duration at ServeHTTP entry, including concurrency admission, decoding/canonicalization, engine-lock wait, and durable inbox commit, and stop after the response encoding attempt. Also record the narrower durable-accept segment around accept/enqueue so decode cost and lock/SQLite contention are separable. Queue delay means durable received_at to the beginning of the per-Tailapp delivery attempt. Delivery currently omits received_at, so carry it from inbox_events; across restart this is a wall-clock approximation, clamp negative values and increment a clock-regression counter. Processing duration should cover Projection.Process plus Complete/Detach settlement if its outcome describes the final attempt; otherwise name it evaluation_commit_duration and add a separate settlement duration. Query duration starts before Engine.mu.Lock and ends after sandbox close/result materialization, so it includes the lock wait users feel. Add query_engine_lock_wait_duration_milliseconds as a separate diagnostic because Engine.Query currently holds the engine mutex throughout open/attach, prepare, row scan, and materialization. Control duration starts at handler entry and ends after response envelope encoding; define that the metrics read operation itself appears only in the next snapshot.\n\nUse one documented fixed bucket vector for every millisecond histogram, for example 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000, 2500, 5000, and 10000, with implicit +Inf. Keep a nanosecond cumulative sum so sub-millisecond work is not rounded to zero.\n\nREQUIRED 3 — make cardinality bounded in lifecycle, not merely in label vocabulary. Signal, outcome, and operation labels are closed enums. Never label by event name, source, session, SQL, revision, path, error message, request ID, trace ID, or span ID. Tailapp name is the sole proposed variable identity and is already visible to the same owner-only CLI/MCP caller, but it is still operator-controlled and may contain sensitive project naming. Keep per-Tailapp series only for currently installed/active definitions, remove their registry entries on delete, and retain separate unlabeled process totals. Do not keep tombstoned per-name series for the process lifetime. If “bounded” must mean a strict numeric bound rather than bounded by current definitions, impose a maximum installed-Tailapp count; otherwise document this one exception precisely.\n\nREQUIRED 4 — add the missing measures that tell an operator whether active analytics are actually useful. Accepted OTLP traffic is not sufficient: with zero healthy consumers, Enqueue advances positions and immediately deletes the source records. Add tailapp_otlp_records_unrouted_total by signal. A deterministic gap, delete, or runtime upgrade can detach many obligations; return/count the number from DetachAll and expose tailapp_projection_obligations_detached_total by active Tailapp and closed reason. Add current inbox_oldest_record_age_milliseconds and per-active-Tailapp frontier_lag_positions (delivery_head minus interpreted_position, explicitly not “pending records”). Expose the already durable per-projection tailapp_stats values consumed_records, ineffective_records, and emitted_events as a separate “since activation/reset” section. These four measures distinguish healthy intake from no consumer, data loss, backlog, and useful fold activity. Query result bytes are also required because the query budget is byte-bounded and row count alone misses wide results.\n\nLOCKING/IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENT: never take a metrics registry mutex while holding Engine.mu, Projection.mu, the mutation mutex, or a SQLite transaction. Register a fixed per-component/per-active-Tailapp handle during lifecycle changes; hot-path observations should be pointer-local atomic increments into fixed arrays, not map lookup or allocation. Snapshot by copying handle pointers under a short registry read lock, release it, then read atomics and acquire Engine.mu/SQLite only for current gauges. Derive histogram count from exclusive atomic buckets or otherwise preserve count/bucket invariants. Use runtime/metrics.Read for heap and goroutine gauges rather than runtime.ReadMemStats on every snapshot. The existing engine mutex is the dominant serialization cost; measurement must reveal its wait time without extending it materially.\n\nADVISORY: report active, unavailable, and upgrade-pending Tailapp counts; delivery obligations created total to show fan-out work; idempotent replay success/error separately from executed mutations; last successful processing timestamp per active Tailapp; and snapshot collection duration. Keep tailapp metrics JSON as the default CLI format to match the existing CLI contract; --json may be accepted as an explicit alias. Document that snapshots are concurrency-safe but not one globally atomic instant across registry, SQLite, and Go runtime gauges.\n\nWith these refinements, the contract stays tiny, privacy-preserving, restart-legible, and directly useful for diagnosing intake pressure, lock contention, projection stalls/loss, and query cost.","body":{"head":"f14a79fdb97e09d791b213b16cec1932d424574a","scope":"design-advice","verdict":"approve-with-required-refinements"}}